WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.313 --> 00:00:02.562
- I want to have dragons and dire wolves

00:00:02.562 --> 00:00:06.702
and mammoths and a wall of
ice that's 700 feet tall.

00:00:06.702 --> 00:00:09.492
A minute ago I don't like stories

00:00:09.492 --> 00:00:12.080
or books that are too
familiar, too predictable,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.172
that I feel like I read
a thousand times before.

00:00:14.172 --> 00:00:16.930
Very few people get up in the morning

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:21.125
and say oh I'm evil,
what evil can I do today?

00:00:21.125 --> 00:00:23.353
It's always said that
no is the sexiest word

00:00:23.353 --> 00:00:24.905
you can say in Hollywood.

00:00:24.905 --> 00:00:27.516
The more you say no
the more they want you.

00:00:27.516 --> 00:00:30.621
- He's an American novelist, screenwriter,

00:00:30.621 --> 00:00:32.083
and TV producter.

00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:35.301
He's best known for his
series A Song of Ice and Fire

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:38.680
which was later turned
into HBO's Game of Thrones.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.344
In 2005 Time Magazine called
him the American Tolkien.

00:00:42.344 --> 00:00:44.909
He's George R. R. Martin
and here is my take

00:00:44.909 --> 00:00:46.777
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:46.777 --> 00:00:48.892
Rule number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:48.892 --> 00:00:50.534
make sure to stick around
all the way to the end

00:00:50.534 --> 00:00:52.358
for some special bonus clips and as always

00:00:52.358 --> 00:00:54.092
as George is talking, if he says something

00:00:54.092 --> 00:00:55.552
that really is meaningful to you,

00:00:55.552 --> 00:00:57.037
that has a big impact on you,

00:00:57.037 --> 00:00:58.567
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:02.734
so others can benefit from
the experience as well. Enjoy.

00:01:05.702 --> 00:01:08.285
(upbeat music)

00:01:15.713 --> 00:01:17.648
- And I started actually
writing a science fiction novel

00:01:17.648 --> 00:01:20.393
that had been an idea I'd
been playing with a long time,

00:01:20.393 --> 00:01:23.499
a book called Avalon, and
I was working on Avalon

00:01:23.499 --> 00:01:27.666
and it was coming pretty well
and suddenly the first chapter

00:01:28.652 --> 00:01:30.518
of Game of Thrones came to me,

00:01:30.518 --> 00:01:33.435
where they find the dire wolf pups.

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:44.479
- It's a freak.

00:01:46.519 --> 00:01:48.342
- It's a dire wolf.

00:01:48.342 --> 00:01:49.558
- I knew it wasn't part of

00:01:49.558 --> 00:01:51.042
the science fiction novel I was writing.

00:01:51.042 --> 00:01:52.864
This was clearly a fantasy, medieval,

00:01:52.864 --> 00:01:55.947
had these wolves and this large family.

00:01:55.947 --> 00:01:58.354
I'd been reading a lot
of historical fiction

00:01:58.354 --> 00:02:00.222
and it might of been
in the back of my mind

00:02:00.222 --> 00:02:02.112
but suddenly it all gelled

00:02:02.112 --> 00:02:05.734
and I wrote that chapter
in like two or three days.

00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:07.917
It just came to pour out of me.

00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:09.000
- Lord Stark.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:15.452
There are five pups, one for
each of the Stark children.

00:02:15.452 --> 00:02:19.126
The dire wolf is an
extension of your house.

00:02:19.126 --> 00:02:21.543
They were meant to have them.

00:02:22.659 --> 00:02:24.685
- You will train them yourselves.

00:02:24.685 --> 00:02:26.306
You will feed them yourselves

00:02:26.306 --> 00:02:30.782
and if they die, you'll
bury them yourselves.

00:02:30.782 --> 00:02:32.604
- When I did return to books,

00:02:32.604 --> 00:02:36.677
I said to myself okay, I've
had many things optioned,

00:02:36.677 --> 00:02:38.095
they never make them.

00:02:38.095 --> 00:02:41.177
I've done pilots and TV
shows, tried to keep them

00:02:41.177 --> 00:02:43.427
within budget and produced
well, they never make that.

00:02:43.427 --> 00:02:44.935
I'm tired of playing that game.

00:02:44.935 --> 00:02:47.005
I'm going to, you know,
I have a big imagination,

00:02:47.005 --> 00:02:48.850
I have an epic story I want to tell.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.628
I'm going to make it as
big as my imagination.

00:02:50.628 --> 00:02:53.890
I'm going to have all the
castles and battles I want.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.848
I'm going to have hundreds of characters

00:02:55.848 --> 00:02:57.243
'cause the real world is complex

00:02:57.243 --> 00:02:59.785
and I want my fictional
world to be complex too,

00:02:59.785 --> 00:03:01.835
not as kind of simplified thing.

00:03:01.835 --> 00:03:03.116
I want to have giant battles.

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:05.208
I want to have incredible castles

00:03:05.208 --> 00:03:07.166
that each one is distinct from the other

00:03:07.166 --> 00:03:09.055
that people will remember for all time.

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:11.261
I want to have dragons and dire wolves

00:03:11.261 --> 00:03:15.537
and mammoths and a wall of
ice that's 700 feet tall.

00:03:15.537 --> 00:03:18.014
I want something huge and epic

00:03:18.014 --> 00:03:20.171
that could stand up there with Tolkien.

00:03:20.171 --> 00:03:24.514
Ask yourself, what if no one
ever bought any of my work?

00:03:24.514 --> 00:03:26.179
What if I'm never going to sell a book

00:03:26.179 --> 00:03:29.172
or publish a book or a short story?

00:03:29.172 --> 00:03:32.142
Are you going to continue to write?

00:03:32.142 --> 00:03:34.437
And if the answer's
yes, because I have to,

00:03:34.437 --> 00:03:36.035
then yeah, then you are a writer

00:03:36.035 --> 00:03:37.836
and whether you'll sell
or not, I can't know

00:03:37.836 --> 00:03:41.167
but if there's stories in
you that have to get out,

00:03:41.167 --> 00:03:42.852
you want to put them down on the paper

00:03:42.852 --> 00:03:44.563
whether you ever get the affirmation

00:03:44.563 --> 00:03:48.095
of a contract or a book in your hand

00:03:48.095 --> 00:03:49.783
or being on the bestseller list,

00:03:49.783 --> 00:03:51.245
if you still have to tell your stories

00:03:51.245 --> 00:03:52.754
then you're a writer.

00:03:52.754 --> 00:03:55.836
If you can give it up,
then it probably would be

00:03:55.836 --> 00:04:00.696
an act of sanity to learn
accounting or something like that.

00:04:00.696 --> 00:04:03.171
I have tons of things I'd like to do

00:04:03.171 --> 00:04:06.389
and life is short,
unfortunately, but I hope

00:04:06.389 --> 00:04:10.799
it will be long enough for
me to do some of these.

00:04:10.799 --> 00:04:14.664
I have known a few
writers who have retired.

00:04:14.664 --> 00:04:16.615
I cannot imagine ever retiring.

00:04:16.615 --> 00:04:20.782
Writing and being a writer is
too much a part of my identity

00:04:22.214 --> 00:04:24.284
and too much a part of what I am.

00:04:24.284 --> 00:04:27.187
I don't know what I would
do with all that time

00:04:27.187 --> 00:04:28.716
if I did stop writing.

00:04:28.716 --> 00:04:30.742
You know, play video games
or something like that.

00:04:30.742 --> 00:04:32.225
Watch a lot more television.

00:04:32.225 --> 00:04:34.545
It seems unlikely to me.

00:04:34.545 --> 00:04:38.256
I've always told stories
whether I could make

00:04:38.256 --> 00:04:41.338
my living from it or even
when I was a little kid

00:04:41.338 --> 00:04:43.003
just making up stories for the other kids

00:04:43.003 --> 00:04:45.478
in the neighborhood and I
think I'm going to continue

00:04:45.478 --> 00:04:47.561
to do that until the end.

00:04:51.597 --> 00:04:54.472
- Quite the little climber aren't you?

00:04:54.472 --> 00:04:56.178
How old are you boy?

00:04:56.178 --> 00:04:57.011
- 10.

00:04:57.956 --> 00:04:58.789
- 10.

00:05:08.329 --> 00:05:09.545
The things I do for love.

00:05:09.545 --> 00:05:12.063
(gasping)

00:05:12.063 --> 00:05:13.729
- I mean not many shows or books

00:05:13.729 --> 00:05:15.912
have the guts to kill a 10 year old.

00:05:15.912 --> 00:05:16.947
(laughing)

00:05:16.947 --> 00:05:21.114
Let alone after him catching
an incestuous relationship.

00:05:22.752 --> 00:05:23.834
- That's true.

00:05:23.834 --> 00:05:26.174
(laughing)

00:05:26.174 --> 00:05:27.683
Probably more surprising in the book

00:05:27.683 --> 00:05:30.585
where of course he's the
first viewpoint character.

00:05:30.585 --> 00:05:32.159
You know, he's the viewpoint character

00:05:32.159 --> 00:05:34.185
of the first chapter
and then we switch way

00:05:34.185 --> 00:05:35.782
a couple of viewpoints
and then come back to him.

00:05:35.782 --> 00:05:39.000
Since he was first I think
the assumption of most readers

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.298
is that he's going to be, you
know, the main guy through that

00:05:43.298 --> 00:05:46.831
and then suddenly you think
he's dead or maybe he's dead.

00:05:46.831 --> 00:05:49.081
But we find out more later.

00:05:50.296 --> 00:05:52.629
But you know it's what I ...

00:05:56.796 --> 00:05:58.712
We write what we want to read

00:05:58.712 --> 00:06:03.394
and I, as I said a minute
ago, I don't like stories

00:06:03.394 --> 00:06:06.071
or books that are too familiar,
that are too predictable,

00:06:06.071 --> 00:06:08.659
that I feel like I read
a thousand times before.

00:06:08.659 --> 00:06:11.494
So I want to keep you
on edge and surprise you

00:06:11.494 --> 00:06:14.688
and make you think that no one is safe

00:06:14.688 --> 00:06:18.761
and that was certainly the intent there.

00:06:18.761 --> 00:06:23.239
And that scene has, I think,
gotten a lot of people

00:06:23.239 --> 00:06:26.052
hooked on these books and said you know

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:28.302
I thought I was reading
just another fantasy

00:06:28.302 --> 00:06:30.349
until I hit that scene and then I realized

00:06:30.349 --> 00:06:32.284
that this was something
a little different.

00:06:32.284 --> 00:06:35.850
So that moment has
worked very well for me.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:38.167
- You are no knight.

00:06:38.167 --> 00:06:40.463
You have forsaken every vow you ever took.

00:06:40.463 --> 00:06:42.285
- So many vows.

00:06:42.285 --> 00:06:44.910
They make you swear and swear.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:48.446
Defend the king, obey the
king, obey your father,

00:06:48.446 --> 00:06:50.799
protect the innocent, defend the weak.

00:06:50.799 --> 00:06:54.949
And what if your father despises the king?

00:06:54.949 --> 00:06:58.954
What if the king massacres the innocent?

00:06:58.954 --> 00:07:00.799
It's too much.

00:07:00.799 --> 00:07:05.232
No matter what you do, you're
forsaking one vow or another.

00:07:05.232 --> 00:07:08.405
- I think the struggle
between good and evil

00:07:08.405 --> 00:07:10.947
is central to fantasy
and indeed in some ways

00:07:10.947 --> 00:07:12.815
central to most fiction.

00:07:12.815 --> 00:07:16.350
It's certainly a worthy
subject for fiction.

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:18.553
But where I think I differ is I regard

00:07:18.553 --> 00:07:20.622
the struggle between good and evil

00:07:20.622 --> 00:07:23.795
as being waged within the
individual human heart.

00:07:23.795 --> 00:07:27.045
It's not waged as fantasy would have it

00:07:28.386 --> 00:07:31.763
where a character like
called the dark lord

00:07:31.763 --> 00:07:33.876
gathers all the evil people together

00:07:33.876 --> 00:07:35.474
and puts them in black clothing

00:07:35.474 --> 00:07:37.817
and you know they're evil
'cause they're really ugly

00:07:37.817 --> 00:07:39.592
and all the good people are handsome

00:07:39.592 --> 00:07:42.338
and they wear white and they
meet on a big battlefield.

00:07:42.338 --> 00:07:45.647
In the real world good and
evil are not that complex.

00:07:45.647 --> 00:07:49.110
Very few people get up in the morning

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:53.294
and say oh I'm evil,
what evil can I do today?

00:07:53.294 --> 00:07:55.275
I'm going to cover the world with darkness

00:07:55.275 --> 00:07:58.245
and my legions of evil will rule all.

00:07:58.245 --> 00:08:00.162
That's silly, you know.

00:08:01.102 --> 00:08:04.715
The greatest monsters of
history, as we look back on them,

00:08:04.715 --> 00:08:07.853
thought they were the heroes of the story.

00:08:07.853 --> 00:08:10.261
You know, the villain is
the hero of the other side

00:08:10.261 --> 00:08:11.723
as sometimes said.

00:08:11.723 --> 00:08:15.806
That doesn't mean that
it's all morally relative.

00:08:17.731 --> 00:08:20.094
That doesn't mean that all
things are equally good and evil.

00:08:20.094 --> 00:08:22.908
I think there is good and
there is evil in the world

00:08:22.908 --> 00:08:25.314
but you know, it's sometimes a struggle

00:08:25.314 --> 00:08:28.554
to tell one from the other
and to make the right choices.

00:08:28.554 --> 00:08:31.017
And all of us, we've always been attracted

00:08:31.017 --> 00:08:34.022
to great characters, maybe
because that's what I see

00:08:34.022 --> 00:08:35.641
when I look around the real world.

00:08:35.641 --> 00:08:36.901
I read about it in history books

00:08:36.901 --> 00:08:39.310
or the news or just people I meet.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:43.247
I mean, all of us have it
within ourselves to be heroes.

00:08:43.247 --> 00:08:47.737
All of us have it within
ourselves to be villains.

00:08:47.737 --> 00:08:50.830
We've all done good things in our lives

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:54.580
and most of us have also
done selfish things,

00:08:55.667 --> 00:08:58.976
cowardly things, things that we're

00:08:58.976 --> 00:09:02.309
ashamed of in later years and to my mind

00:09:03.453 --> 00:09:06.783
that's, I don't know, the
glory of the human race.

00:09:06.783 --> 00:09:10.563
We're such wonderfully
contradictory, mixed up creatures

00:09:10.563 --> 00:09:13.894
that are endlessly fascinating

00:09:13.894 --> 00:09:15.604
to write about and read about.

00:09:15.604 --> 00:09:19.924
David Benioff and Dan
Weiss are the show runners.

00:09:19.924 --> 00:09:22.242
You know, I don't have any veto power.

00:09:22.242 --> 00:09:25.527
I signed a pretty standard contract

00:09:25.527 --> 00:09:28.694
where I gave them rights to adapt this

00:09:29.644 --> 00:09:32.255
into a television series
and I got certain titles

00:09:32.255 --> 00:09:36.755
and the agreement I'd
write one script a year

00:09:36.755 --> 00:09:39.367
and a large dump truck full of money.

00:09:39.367 --> 00:09:41.571
(laughing)

00:09:41.571 --> 00:09:43.738
And they can do, you know,

00:09:44.743 --> 00:09:47.041
they can have the aliens
come down next season.

00:09:47.041 --> 00:09:50.624
They can turn the whole
cast into vampires.

00:09:53.126 --> 00:09:55.233
And I'm powerless to stop them

00:09:55.233 --> 00:09:57.753
but I don't think they will do that.

00:09:57.753 --> 00:10:01.871
They love the books
and they seem committed

00:10:01.871 --> 00:10:04.706
to telling my story in a different medium.

00:10:04.706 --> 00:10:07.676
And I knew all that before I
signed any of the contracts.

00:10:07.676 --> 00:10:10.556
I mean, when these books started hitting

00:10:10.556 --> 00:10:12.649
the New York Times Bestseller List

00:10:12.649 --> 00:10:14.538
which was as early as Clash of Kings,

00:10:14.538 --> 00:10:18.297
I was approached by other
people who wanted to adapt them,

00:10:18.297 --> 00:10:23.269
many for feature films and I
had meetings with those people

00:10:23.269 --> 00:10:25.159
and you know, heard their plans.

00:10:25.159 --> 00:10:27.657
How were they going to fit this
giant thing in a feature film?

00:10:27.657 --> 00:10:29.727
Well, we're going to make
it all about Jon Snow.

00:10:29.727 --> 00:10:31.594
We'll drop all these other characters

00:10:31.594 --> 00:10:33.417
or we'll make it all about Dany

00:10:33.417 --> 00:10:35.240
and we'll drop all these other characters.

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:38.705
They had various schemes
of how they would do it.

00:10:38.705 --> 00:10:42.872
Or well, we'll just make the
first book up to this point

00:10:43.996 --> 00:10:47.030
and then you know, we hope that the movie

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:49.415
will do well enough that
they'll order a second movie

00:10:49.415 --> 00:10:51.598
and none of these really appealed to me.

00:10:51.598 --> 00:10:54.931
So I said no, which is, it's always said

00:10:57.515 --> 00:11:00.531
that no is the sexiest word
you could say in Hollywood.

00:11:00.531 --> 00:11:03.208
The more you say no
the more they want you.

00:11:03.208 --> 00:11:05.458
(laughing)

00:11:06.358 --> 00:11:08.406
I guess that was true
'cause they kept coming

00:11:08.406 --> 00:11:10.701
and eventually David and Dan came

00:11:10.701 --> 00:11:13.041
and we had a wonderful meeting

00:11:13.041 --> 00:11:14.729
that lasted like most of the day.

00:11:14.729 --> 00:11:17.992
We met for lunch and we were talking

00:11:17.992 --> 00:11:20.061
and getting animated about how we were

00:11:20.061 --> 00:11:21.794
going to do the series over lunch

00:11:21.794 --> 00:11:23.662
in a crowded restaurant in Los Angeles.

00:11:23.662 --> 00:11:25.349
Little by little it emptied out

00:11:25.349 --> 00:11:27.262
and pretty soon we were
the only people there,

00:11:27.262 --> 00:11:29.129
you know, drinking our seventh
cup of coffee and iced tea

00:11:29.129 --> 00:11:31.245
and still talking about it.

00:11:31.245 --> 00:11:33.900
And then more hours passed
and we're still talking

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:36.240
and the restaurant
dinner crowd is coming in

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:38.468
and they're setting up for dinner.

00:11:38.468 --> 00:11:40.448
I think we closed that
restaurant that night.

00:11:40.448 --> 00:11:43.239
So it was one of those classic meetings

00:11:43.239 --> 00:11:45.578
that you only get once in awhile

00:11:45.578 --> 00:11:47.490
but I had a great feeling about them.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:50.101
You know, if you're J. K. Rowling

00:11:50.101 --> 00:11:52.463
you can go into a situation
where every studio

00:11:52.463 --> 00:11:54.580
in Hollywood wants you and you can set

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:57.618
very stringent terms where
you get to approve everything.

00:11:57.618 --> 00:12:00.091
But if you're not J. K. Rowling

00:12:00.091 --> 00:12:02.837
and virtually nobody is J. K. Rowling

00:12:02.837 --> 00:12:06.954
except for J. K. Rowling,
then you can't do that

00:12:06.954 --> 00:12:09.542
and you have to find people that you trust

00:12:09.542 --> 00:12:11.861
and put your faith in them

00:12:11.861 --> 00:12:14.763
and in the understanding of the story.

00:12:14.763 --> 00:12:17.439
I think I was a sophomore in high school

00:12:17.439 --> 00:12:20.184
and I was kind of a nerdy kid, you know.

00:12:20.184 --> 00:12:22.052
I wasn't one of the cool kids.

00:12:22.052 --> 00:12:23.560
I wasn't good at sports.

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:26.057
I was good at, you know,
honor roll and stuff

00:12:26.057 --> 00:12:29.474
but that doesn't cut any slack in school.

00:12:30.325 --> 00:12:32.113
That just gets you more abuse.

00:12:32.113 --> 00:12:34.073
(laughing)

00:12:34.073 --> 00:12:37.906
But, sophomore year one
of my English teachers

00:12:38.758 --> 00:12:41.540
was teaching Poe's Pit and the Pendulum

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:43.970
and he said, you know, this
story has a terrible ending.

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:46.018
If you remember that story,
the guy's in the pit,

00:12:46.018 --> 00:12:47.727
the pendulum coming lower and lower

00:12:47.727 --> 00:12:49.640
and then suddenly in the last paragraph,

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:52.678
trumpet sound and the pendulum
pulls up and he's saved.

00:12:52.678 --> 00:12:54.860
The inquisition is in
the hands of its enemies

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:56.706
is the last thing and you know,

00:12:56.706 --> 00:12:58.640
my English teacher said this is

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:01.476
a classic example of the Deus Ex Machina.

00:13:01.476 --> 00:13:04.718
So our assignment was
to write a better ending

00:13:04.718 --> 00:13:06.088
for the Pit and the Pendulum.

00:13:06.088 --> 00:13:07.090
- [Man] Oh boy.

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:10.813
- So, I wrote an ending
where the rats came out

00:13:10.813 --> 00:13:13.536
and ate the guy and they ate his cheeks

00:13:13.536 --> 00:13:16.732
and they ate his toes
and they ate his face.

00:13:16.732 --> 00:13:18.871
And the pendulum cut him in half

00:13:18.871 --> 00:13:21.794
and he died horribly,
screaming in horrible pain.

00:13:21.794 --> 00:13:23.662
I thought it was a much better ending

00:13:23.662 --> 00:13:26.249
than the one that Edgar
Allen Poe came up with.

00:13:26.249 --> 00:13:27.982
- It has a certain uplift to it, I feel.

00:13:27.982 --> 00:13:30.187
- It does.
(laughing)

00:13:30.187 --> 00:13:32.032
But what was the real boost for me

00:13:32.032 --> 00:13:33.810
is the teacher liked it too and he had me

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:36.401
read it to the class and suddenly

00:13:36.401 --> 00:13:40.358
all these kids who'd
never thought much of me

00:13:40.358 --> 00:13:42.789
thought I was the
coolest kid in class man.

00:13:42.789 --> 00:13:45.848
Wow, what a great ... (laughing)

00:13:45.848 --> 00:13:47.431
What a great story.

00:13:48.413 --> 00:13:49.651
The rats ate the guy.

00:13:49.651 --> 00:13:51.811
They loved it and I said oh,

00:13:51.811 --> 00:13:53.251
I got to do more of this writing stuff.

00:13:53.251 --> 00:13:55.569
It really pays off.

00:13:55.569 --> 00:13:57.976
When you're writing fantasy,
I've never obviously

00:13:57.976 --> 00:14:00.091
been a prince, I've never been a king.

00:14:00.091 --> 00:14:02.711
I've never murdered anyone.

00:14:02.711 --> 00:14:04.312
I'd never been a dwarf.

00:14:04.312 --> 00:14:06.067
I've never been an 11 year old girl.

00:14:06.067 --> 00:14:09.420
So the question is how do
you write these characters?

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:13.291
Well, there's a certain amount of stuff

00:14:13.291 --> 00:14:16.541
that you have to get by doing research.

00:14:17.476 --> 00:14:19.748
You have to talk to people who actually

00:14:19.748 --> 00:14:21.796
have had these experiences.

00:14:21.796 --> 00:14:25.284
Like when Bran was crippled by his fall,

00:14:25.284 --> 00:14:29.333
I had a couple correspondences
with readers of mine

00:14:29.333 --> 00:14:33.226
who were paraplegic and
had no use of their legs

00:14:33.226 --> 00:14:35.837
and you know described to
me some of the consequences,

00:14:35.837 --> 00:14:37.727
some of the things I
might not have thought of

00:14:37.727 --> 00:14:40.066
and made suggestions to me
of how better to write that

00:14:40.066 --> 00:14:42.609
and I certainly took that
sort of stuff into account.

00:14:42.609 --> 00:14:45.737
But the main thing is
empathy, is saying well okay,

00:14:45.737 --> 00:14:48.550
how would I feel 'cause the
character is still a person.

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:50.755
There's a certain basis
of common humanity.

00:14:50.755 --> 00:14:53.950
And that's true when writing about women

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:57.482
or writing about a dwarf or
writing about any of those.

00:14:57.482 --> 00:14:59.081
You have to start with the basis,

00:14:59.081 --> 00:15:01.758
they're more like me
than they are unlike me.

00:15:01.758 --> 00:15:02.759
- [Man] Right.

00:15:02.759 --> 00:15:04.370
- They have some special condition

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:07.316
that they have a different
set of genitals than I do

00:15:07.316 --> 00:15:10.083
but it's still the same old story,

00:15:10.083 --> 00:15:11.883
a fight for love and glory right?

00:15:11.883 --> 00:15:12.885
- [Man] Right.

00:15:12.885 --> 00:15:15.034
- We all want kind of the
same things out of life

00:15:15.034 --> 00:15:18.611
and that humanity can motivate characters

00:15:18.611 --> 00:15:20.591
and make them real and that's what I try

00:15:20.591 --> 00:15:21.942
to remember at all times.

00:15:21.942 --> 00:15:23.674
When you write, when I write anyway,

00:15:23.674 --> 00:15:26.674
I wan to engage my readers' emotions

00:15:28.692 --> 00:15:30.582
or my viewers' emotions.

00:15:30.582 --> 00:15:35.105
You don't want, you know,
death is a big thing.

00:15:35.105 --> 00:15:37.480
When we experience death in real life,

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:41.855
it effects us, you
know, when a spouse dies

00:15:41.855 --> 00:15:45.105
or your parents die, your brother dies,

00:15:46.265 --> 00:15:48.539
your dog gets run over or God forbid

00:15:48.539 --> 00:15:51.396
one of your children
dies, rips your heart out.

00:15:51.396 --> 00:15:53.646
It effects you emotionally.

00:15:55.206 --> 00:15:58.244
If we're creating characters
that we really care about,

00:15:58.244 --> 00:16:00.787
we should react in a
similar way when they die

00:16:00.787 --> 00:16:03.622
in fiction or on television.

00:16:03.622 --> 00:16:05.917
It shouldn't be oh okay,
that character's dead.

00:16:05.917 --> 00:16:08.324
Let me go out and get a
sandwich during the commercial.

00:16:08.324 --> 00:16:11.137
I mean, then you're not doing your job.

00:16:11.137 --> 00:16:13.050
I want to grab hold of my readers

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:16.109
and make them cry and make them grieve

00:16:16.109 --> 00:16:19.125
and make them laugh as
well and experience joy,

00:16:19.125 --> 00:16:21.083
experience the worlds on the page

00:16:21.083 --> 00:16:22.928
as if they were living them.

00:16:22.928 --> 00:16:25.763
That's the dream of me at least

00:16:25.763 --> 00:16:27.541
and I think of most writers.

00:16:27.541 --> 00:16:30.175
- How did you decide on the name Hodor?

00:16:30.175 --> 00:16:34.342
(laughing)
(clapping)

00:16:39.966 --> 00:16:43.070
- That's not his real name,
that's just what they call him.

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:44.603
(laughing)

00:16:44.603 --> 00:16:46.197
His real name is Walter.

00:16:46.197 --> 00:16:47.773
If you were named Walter you might want

00:16:47.773 --> 00:16:49.842
to be called Hodor too.

00:16:49.842 --> 00:16:52.140
I don't know, you have to keep reading.

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:54.557
